00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.120
Speaker 2: Peace initiative, Mujahideen.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.519
Speaker 1: So what's going on?

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:10.519
This group.

00:03:10.519 --> 00:03:11.879
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:16.389
It seems like in the U.S., the folks that
are concerned about climate change are in

00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:20.199
the minority when it comes to affecting public
policy.

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:25.689
How can we overcome the overwhelming political
odds to pass meaningful legislation?

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:32.079
And how can we how can we do a quick, quick
enough to hopefully avoid the worst effects

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:33.809
of climate change that are predicted?

00:03:33.809 --> 00:03:36.419
Speaker 1: I don't know that I agree with
the premise.

00:03:36.419 --> 00:03:38.359
Now, let's let's see if we can clarify it.

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:43.510
You're saying most of the general public doesn't
want to do anything about climate change or

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.760
are most of our elected officials don't?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.510
Speaker 2: I think I think elected officials
is like all the money that comes from oil

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.599
companies and all of that.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:56.049
Speaker 1: So here's the thing.

00:03:56.049 --> 00:04:00.339
Most of the top of the population does want
to do something about climate change.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:03.479
So the solution is we need that.

00:04:03.479 --> 00:04:05.379
This is a crazy radical idea.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:09.659
We need to elect people who will do the will
of the voters.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:11.989
And I know it's like so quaint.

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:19.299
The problem, of course, is between gerrymandering
and campaign finance and super PACs and all

00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:24.099
